Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:07.120
Hello everyone! I'm Alicia Malone 
This is Fandango's Indie Movie Guide

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:11.340
There are so many indies out this week but I wanna concentrate on two of them

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:14.740
These two movies are so brilliant, so absorbing...

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:19.100
and are definetly gonna end up on my "Best" list of 2016

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:23.280
Enough teasing Malone!
Ok, fair enough. Let's get into it with 'Moonlight'

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:29.900
-*Clip* -Why you're looking at me like that?

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:33.320
-Like what, man? Come on, you just drove down here...?

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:37.020
-Yeah...

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:44.540
Before I even get started with this one I'm gonna put a big "awards watch" off the top

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:46.700
because this definitely deserves it.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:52.060
You may have heard me talking about 'Moonlight' in my episode I did from TIFF

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:53.420
But just as a reminder

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:58.760
'Moonlight' is directed by Barry Jerkins, and it tells the story of one man's life in three different parts

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:03.740
So first you start up with him as a child, then you jump to him as a teenager,

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:06.400
and then jump again to him as an older man

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:08.220
And he is dealing with so much stuff.

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:11.340
trying to find a father figure, his crack addict mother

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:16.520
getting beating up at school and also battleling with figuring out his own sexuality

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:20.140
-At some point you gotta decide for yourself who you wanna be

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:22.440
-Can't let nobody make that decision for you

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:27.140
'Moonlight' has this dreamlike, memory-type quality

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:30.320
and Barry Jenkins has crafted a beautifully understated

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:34.420
yet still emotionally epic and boundary-pushing story

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:39.680
I've said before that 'Moonlight' is kind of like a meditation of masculinity

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:43.000
and what I mean by that is that gently and poetically

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:45.900
explores the ideas of what it means to be a man

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:49.700
and the boxes you're supposed to fit in to, 
or the act you're suppose to play

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:51.560
You know the strenght and the violence

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:56.080
can sometimes be a mask for what it's really happening inside, the inner emotional thermboil

00:01:57.420 --> 00:01:59.420
*clip* -What's wrong?
-I'm good

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:03.540
-No, I'm seeing you ain't

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:08.700
Moolight is definitely #AliciaApproves

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:10.860
Try to see it if it's playing in your area

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:14.680
because this is the kind of small movie that 
absolutely deserves our support

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:17.080
-I ain't see you like a dad

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:20.400
-It's not what I expected

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:24.280
-What did you expect?

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:30.120
Moving on to my other big recommendation this week

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:32.860
'The handmaiden' by the genious: Park Chan-Wook

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:51.580
So I would put an "awards watch" over this one

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:55.680
but because it would end up in the Best Foreign Language category at the Oscars

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:59.880
and for that to happen It would need to be submitted via South Korea

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:01.680
as being they're official entry

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:06.100
and South Korea has chosen 'The Age of Shadows' as their official entry

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:11.240
So... Oh, well... I'm sure it's way to controversial for the Academy anyways

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:16.800
because this is an erotic-lesbian gothic-heist thriller

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:19.700
Japanese-Korean movie

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:34.580
I saw this movie in Cannes and the crazy story kept me at the edge of my seat

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:37.420
for the full 2 hours and 45 minutes

00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:39.660
It's also sumptously shot

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:44.260
It's gorgeous to look at, 
almost fetishly film at some points

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:48.880
Yes, it is very erotic, so it definitely is R rated

00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:51.620
Don't take your kids, don't go with your parents

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:54.320
The two lead momen are fantastic

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:56.800
and you know ever since 'Sympathy for Lady Vengeance'

00:03:56.800 --> 00:04:01.000
Park Chan-Wook has really been focusing on these strong female central characters

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:05.720
and when I was at Fantastic Fest I got to sit down with the genius director himself

00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:10.900
So, I wanted to know why he's changed his focus to women in the last few movies

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:15.100
By the way, I shot this interview myself... so apologies if it doesn't look that good

00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:16.360
Take it away Park Chan-Wook

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:18.400
Can I get a woop-woop? No?
Ok, then

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:24.500
So to recap: my picks this week are the emotionally satisfying 'Moonlight' by Barry Jenkins

00:05:24.920 --> 00:05:28.980
Or the twisty korean thriller: 'The Handmaiden' by Park Chan-Wook

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:31.200
Stay tune to the very end of this episode

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:34.620
to see my other 3 favourite films by Park Chan-Wook

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:38.360
All of these are available on FandangoNOW for you to watch at home

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:41.040
I'm Alicia Malone. I'll see you next week
Bye!

